FILE: vUX-modalDisplayer.js
FEATURE: modal-scroll-lock
DATE: 2026-08-03
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'modal-stacking'

================================================================================
CHANGE 1 — Hold the page with overflow:hidden instead of position:fixed
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
var pageState = { scrollY: 0, bodyPosition: "", frozen: false };

...

function freezePage() {
    if (pageState.frozen) return; //only the bottom layer freezes; nested opens must not re-capture
    pageState.scrollY = window.scrollY;
    pageState.bodyPosition = document.body.style["position"];
    document.body.style["position"] = "fixed";
    document.body.style["top"] = "-" + pageState.scrollY + "px";
    pageState.frozen = true;
}

function releasePage() {
    if (!pageState.frozen) return;
    document.body.style["position"] = pageState.bodyPosition;
    document.body.style["top"] = "";
    pageState.frozen = false;

    //jump back instantly, then leave the page's own scroll-behavior as it was
    var htmlEle = $$.ss("html");
    var previousScrollBehavior = htmlEle.style["scroll-behavior"];
    htmlEle.style["scroll-behavior"] = "auto";
    scrollTo(0, pageState.scrollY);
    htmlEle.style["scroll-behavior"] = previousScrollBehavior;
}

NEW_SECTION
var pageState = { bodyOverflow: "", frozen: false };

...

//The page is held still with overflow rather than by taking the body out of flow:
//the document never moves, so the scroll position needs no capturing and no
//restoring, and the body keeps its own layout while a modal is up.
function freezePage() {
    if (pageState.frozen) return; //only the bottom layer freezes; nested opens must not re-freeze
    pageState.bodyOverflow = document.body.style["overflow"];
    document.body.style["overflow"] = "hidden";
    pageState.frozen = true;
}

function releasePage() {
    if (!pageState.frozen) return;
    document.body.style["overflow"] = pageState.bodyOverflow;
    pageState.frozen = false;
}

REASON
Requested change of mechanism. It turns out to fix a significant layout defect as
well, and it removes a whole category of state.

The layout defect, measured on the example page at a 1280px viewport: setting
`body { position: fixed }` takes the body out of normal flow, at which point it has
no containing block width and collapses to shrink-to-fit. Content width behind the
modal went from 1217px to 528.5px — a 688px reflow of the entire page every time a
modal opened, and back again on close. Under the new mechanism the same measurement
shifts by 15px, which is the width of the scrollbar being removed and is unavoidable
for any scroll lock that hides the scrollbar. This was previously recorded as a
known limitation of the position:fixed approach; it is now fixed.

The state removal follows from the mechanism. Taking the body out of flow makes the
document jump to the top, so the old code had to capture window.scrollY on open and
scrollTo() it back on close — and, because a consumer page with
`scroll-behavior: smooth` would animate that jump, it had to suppress and restore
scroll-behavior on <html> around the call. With overflow the document never moves,
so pageState.scrollY, the top offset, the scrollTo and the entire scroll-behavior
dance all disappear. releasePage() no longer touches <html> at all. The scroll
position is now preserved because nothing scrolled, rather than because it was saved
and reapplied — which is strictly more robust, since there is no window in which the
saved value can be wrong.

What is captured and restored is the body's own inline overflow value, so a page
that already set `body { overflow: scroll }` gets that back rather than having the
property cleared.

BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: `overflow: hidden` blocks user scrolling (wheel, touch, keyboard)
but does not block programmatic scrolling — `scrollTo()` from consumer code will
still move the page behind an open modal. The previous mechanism blocked that too,
as a side effect of the document collapsing to viewport height. This is documented
rather than worked around: suppressing programmatic scrolling would mean fighting
the consumer's own code.

KNOWN LIMITATION, unchanged in kind but worth restating: iOS Safari ignores
`overflow: hidden` on body for scroll locking, where the position:fixed technique
does work. Mobile Safari is the one environment where the old approach was better.

================================================================================
CHANGE 2 — Correct a now-stale ordering comment
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
    freezePage(); //must run before the overlay goes up, while the page scroll is still readable

NEW_SECTION
    freezePage(); //no-op for every layer after the first

REASON
The comment described a constraint that no longer exists. freezePage() used to read
window.scrollY, which had to happen before anything disturbed the page, so its
position in openLayer() was load-bearing. It now only swaps an overflow value and
can run at any point. The replacement comment states what is actually worth knowing
at the call site, which is that the call is a no-op for nested opens.
